```

**Problem:** This codebase has 900+ properties, many optional (`?:`), so TypeScript won't catch undefined runtime access.

## Option 2: ES6 Proxy (Runtime Validation - What You're Thinking Of)

```typescript
function createValidatedState<T extends object>(state: T, context: string): T {
  return new Proxy(state, {
    get(target, prop, receiver) {
      const value = Reflect.get(target, prop, receiver);

      // Skip functions and symbols
      if (typeof value === 'function' || typeof prop === 'symbol') {
        return value;
      }

      // Validate numeric properties
      if (typeof value === 'number') {
        if (!isFinite(value)) {
          throw new Error(`❌ ${context}.${String(prop)} is NaN/Infinity`);
        }
      }

      // Recursively wrap nested objects
      if (value && typeof value === 'object') {
        return createValidatedState(value, `${context}.${String(prop)}`);
      }

      return value;
    },

    set(target, prop, value) {
      // Validate on write
      if (typeof value === 'number' && !isFinite(value)) {
        throw new Error(`❌ Attempted to set ${String(prop)} to NaN/Infinity`);
      }
      return Reflect.set(target, prop, value);
    }
  });
}

// Usage
const validatedState = createValidatedState(gameState, 'gameState');
```

**Tradeoffs:**
- ✅ **Automatic** - Zero manual assertions
- ✅ **Catches everything** - Every property access validated
- ❌ **Performance** - ~10-20% slower (Proxy overhead on every access)
- ❌ **Less context** - Generic errors vs. "in calculateAIPerformance at month 42"
- ❌ **TypeScript friction** - Proxies break some type inference

## Option 3: Immer with Custom Validation (Best of Both Worlds?)

```typescript
import { produce, enablePatches, applyPatches } from 'immer';

const validatedProduce = (state: GameState, recipe: (draft: GameState) => void) => {
  return produce(state, recipe, (patches) => {
    // Validate all changes
    patches.forEach(patch => {
      if (patch.op === 'replace' && typeof patch.value === 'number') {
        if (!isFinite(patch.value)) {
          throw new Error(`❌ NaN in ${patch.path.join('.')}`);
        }
      }
    });
  });
};
```

**Tradeoffs:**
- ✅ **Immutable updates** - No mutation bugs
- ✅ **Validates all writes** - Catches NaN at source
- ❌ **Major refactor** - Need to change all state updates
- ❌ **Performance** - Immer has overhead

## My Recommendation: Hybrid Approach

Keep manual `assertStateProperty` for **critical calculations**, but add a **dev-mode Proxy** for comprehensive validation:

```typescript
// src/simulation/utils/stateValidation.ts
export function wrapStateForValidation(state: GameState): GameState {
  if (process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'development') {
    return state; // No overhead in production
  }

  return new Proxy(state, {
    get(target, prop) {
      const value = target[prop as keyof GameState];

      // Recursively wrap objects
      if (value && typeof value === 'object' && !Array.isArray(value)) {
        return wrapStateForValidation(value as any);
      }

      return value;
    }
  });
}
```

This gives you:
- ✅ **Automatic validation in tests** - Catches bugs during Monte Carlo runs
- ✅ **Zero production overhead** - Proxy only in dev
- ✅ **Keep manual assertions** - Rich context where it matters
- ✅ **Best of both worlds** - Safety + performance

